      {
        "ContentType": "Description",
        "SortOrder": 0,
        "Content": "Gallagher Quigley are privileged to bring to the market one of the peninsula’s most attractive period homes for sale. \r\n\r\n“The Manse” is a home of historic craftsmanship from an era when buildings were conceived with genuine ambition and created to endure. \r\n\r\nOriginally commissioned at the close of the nineteenth century under a lease granted by the Earl of Howth, this remarkable detached residence was designed by a British architect selected through open competition, with a brief to deliver a dwelling of substance and permanence. The winning design drew deeply from the Arts & Crafts movement enriched by strong neo-Gothic influences. The result is a home of extraordinary character & charm that has stood the test of time admirably. \r\n\r\nThe sandstone mullioned windows, steeply pitched red-tiled roof and broad hinged front door are just some of the aesthetically pleasing details that greet on arrival. Inside, the architectural features will delight viewers as they are greeted by a wood-panelled porch leading to a tiled, arched hallway. The generous staircase rises with high balustrades, and feature fireplaces anchor each principal room. \r\n\r\nUpstairs, the barrel and cathedral ceilings are complemented by the window details and painted timber finishes that frame each space. Throughout, natural hardwood timber floors and extra-high ceilings lend the interiors a genuine warmth while generous proportions throughout provide a depth of endearing space. The accommodation comprising c.243.7M2 (2,622 square feet) is both substantial and versatile with four bedrooms, three bathrooms, three reception rooms and a large kitchen that sits at the heart of the home. To the rear, a south-facing garden with planted borders catches the sun from morning to evening, with the beautiful church building providing a delightful backdrop, a setting, it is fair to state, that is entirely unique to this exceptional home. \r\n\r\nA freshwater well, discovered during renovation, has been thoughtfully incorporated as a garden feature, a quiet nod to the self-sufficiency of another age. The Manse is an elegant listed home passed from church to private ownership in 2008. Since then, it has benefited from a careful and considered renovation, one that respects every layer of its heritage while delivering the comfort of a modern family home. \r\n\r\nIt is interesting to note the adjacent church tower was the very place where four young men were permitted to rehearse. U2 were nurtured and encouraged here, a charming detail that speaks to the warmth and spirit of this community. Despite its sense of seclusion and tranquillity, the Manse is convenient to all local amenities. Howth Dart station is within easy walking distance, placing the city centre comfortably within reach in under thirty minutes. The village with its celebrated harbour, restaurants, coastal walks and weekend markets is moments away, while the broader road network via Sutton Cross provides swift access to the M50 and Dublin Airport beyond. \r\n\r\nThe Manse is that rare combination: a home that feels a world apart yet is connected to everything that matters. For those seeking a unique home of remarkable character and charm, this is quite an exceptional opportunity."
      },
